mio sys/unix/selector/kqueue.rs Selector::try_clone: Mac OS X before 10.7 has no F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC (67, xnu-1699+),
and xnu-1504 (10.6) answers an unknown fcntl command on a kqueue with EBADF, so tokio's runtime builder (the io
driver clones mio's selector, so does mio's kqueue Waker) died with "Failed building the Runtime: Os { code: 9,
kind: Uncategorized, message: \"Bad file descriptor\" }" (jlq 0.1.0: tokio 1.21.2, mio 0.8.5). The std patch
rustc-1.90.0-macos-legacy-dupfd-cloexec.patch covers mio 1.0+, which clones through std's OwnedFd::try_clone; mio
0.7.12..0.8.11 call fcntl(F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC) themselves (0.7.11 and earlier use dup, which works). Duplicate with F_DUPFD and set
FD_CLOEXEC afterwards, as the std patch does (not atomic against a concurrent fork + exec).
Base: mio 0.8.5 (0.7.13 introduced LOWEST_FD; the hunk applies to 0.7.13..0.8.11).

--- a/src/sys/unix/selector/kqueue.rs	2006-07-24 01:21:28.000000000 +0000
+++ b/src/sys/unix/selector/kqueue.rs	2026-10-21 08:46:12.916719013 +0000
@@ -75,7 +75,18 @@
     }
 
     pub fn try_clone(&self) -> io::Result<Selector> {
-        syscall!(fcntl(self.kq, libc::F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, super::LOWEST_FD)).map(|kq| Selector {
+        // Mac OS X before 10.7 has no F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC (67), and its kernel answers an unknown fcntl command on a
+        // kqueue with EBADF (tokio: "Failed building the Runtime: Bad file descriptor"). Duplicate with F_DUPFD and
+        // set FD_CLOEXEC afterwards, as std does there.
+        syscall!(fcntl(self.kq, libc::F_DUPFD, super::LOWEST_FD))
+            .and_then(|kq| match syscall!(fcntl(kq, libc::F_SETFD, libc::FD_CLOEXEC)) {
+                Ok(_) => Ok(kq),
+                Err(err) => {
+                    let _ = unsafe { libc::close(kq) };
+                    Err(err)
+                }
+            })
+            .map(|kq| Selector {
             // It's the same selector, so we use the same id.
             #[cfg(debug_assertions)]
             id: self.id,
